    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d690">

                <pb facs="0837" n="743" sameAs="frus:frus1896/pg743"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Springer to Mr. Rockhill.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >United States Consulate-General</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Habana</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1896-11-20"><hi rendition="#italic">November 20,
                        1896</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 223.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to inform the Department
                    that on the 14th instant I received from Mrs. Emma Laborde, of Key West, a copy,
                    certified by the collector of customs of that port, of the oath and appointment
                    of her husband as master of the American schooner <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Competitor.</hi></p>
                <p>I returned this certificate to the collector, with the request that he procure
                    the authentication of his signature by the Spanish consul, believing that the
                    document would be more valid before the court here, and also forestall any
                    objection that might be offered for the want of such formality.</p>
                <p>The certificate has been returned duly indorsed, and I have transmitted it to the
                    Governor-General, with the request that it be forwarded to the court having
                    cognizance of Mr. Laborde’s case.</p>
                <p>In her letter, Mrs. Laborde states that she had been informed by the <pb
                        facs="0838" n="744" xml:id="pg744"/> collector that the Spanish consul had
                    also obtained a certificate regarding her husband, and the port he had cleared
                    for, Miami.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Joseph A.
                    Springer.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
